Cognizant DN 5.0 Java FSE
Week 2 – Spring Data JPA & Hibernate
HQL, JPQL, Native Query & Criteria Query Hands-ons
Objective

The objective of these hands-on exercises is to understand and implement Hibernate Query Language (HQL), Java Persistence Query Language (JPQL), Native SQL Queries, and Criteria Queries using Spring Data JPA and Hibernate. These exercises demonstrate how Hibernate interacts with relational databases while working with Java entity objects instead of directly writing SQL statements.

Hands-on 1 – Introduction to HQL and JPQL
Objective

To understand the basics of Hibernate Query Language (HQL) and Java Persistence Query Language (JPQL).

Concepts Learned
Introduction to HQL
Introduction to JPQL
Difference between SQL, HQL and JPQL
Object-oriented querying using entity classes
HQL supports SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE and INSERT operations.
JPQL is a subset of HQL and supports SELECT, UPDATE and DELETE operations.
Outcome

Understood how HQL and JPQL operate on Java entity classes instead of database tables.

Hands-on 2 – Get All Permanent Employees using HQL
Objective

Retrieve all permanent employees using Hibernate Query Language.

Concepts Implemented
Custom HQL query using @Query annotation
Repository layer implementation
Service layer implementation
Application testing using Spring Boot
Fetching associated Department and Skill entities
Understanding FetchType.EAGER and FetchType.LAZY
Optimizing HQL using JOIN FETCH
Reducing unnecessary SQL queries
Outcome

Successfully retrieved all permanent employees along with their associated departments and skills using optimized HQL queries.

Hands-on 3 – Fetch Quiz Attempt Details using HQL
Objective

Retrieve complete quiz attempt details including user information, questions, options, selected answers and scores.

Concepts Implemented
Complex HQL joins
One-to-One, One-to-Many and Many-to-One mappings
Multiple entity relationships
JOIN FETCH usage
Nested entity traversal
Displaying structured quiz attempt details
Additional Note

The original learning materials referenced a quiz.mwb (MySQL Workbench Model) file for generating the SQL schema.

However, this file was not provided in the official learning materials.

To proceed with the hands-on as intended, a custom dummy quiz database schema and sample dataset were created based on the schema diagram and requirements described in the handout. This allowed successful implementation and testing of all required HQL queries while preserving the intended functionality of the exercise.

Hands-on 4 – Get Average Salary using HQL
Objective

Calculate the average salary of employees in a department using HQL aggregate functions.

Concepts Implemented
AVG() aggregate function
Parameter binding using @Param
Dynamic HQL parameters
Aggregate functions in Hibernate
Outcome

Successfully computed the average salary for a selected department.

Hands-on 5 – Get All Employees using Native Query
Objective

Execute a native SQL query using Spring Data JPA.

Concepts Implemented
Native SQL Query
nativeQuery = true
Repository implementation
Service implementation
Displaying employee information
Outcome

Successfully retrieved all employee records using direct SQL instead of HQL.

Hands-on 6 – Criteria Query
Objective

Understand the concept and importance of Hibernate Criteria Query.

Concepts Learned
Dynamic query generation
Programmatic query creation
Runtime filtering
Dynamic WHERE clause generation
Real-world filtering examples such as Amazon product search
Outcome

Understood how Criteria Query simplifies dynamic searching where query conditions depend on user-selected filters.

Note: This hands-on was theory-based only and did not require any code implementation according to the provided learning material.

